  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 121 PM EST Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: The unsettled weather continues this afternoon as \nanother shortwave moves through the region. The greatest chances for \nprecipitation will be across the Adirondacks and northern Greens, \nparticularly in the favored upslope locations. There may be enough \ninstability to allow for a few heavier downpours or even some \ngraupel, but for the most part light rainfall is expected with snow \nlevels near summit level. Some of the upslope showers may linger \ninto tomorrow morning, especially across the Northeast Kingdom, but \noverall tomorrow will feature drier weather ahead of the next system \narriving late week. Temperatures to be rather seasonable through \ntomorrow, with daytime highs in the upper 30s to near 50 and \novernight lows generally in the 30s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2:  Confidence continues to increase in the likelihood of \nwidespread precipitation across the region Thursday into Friday. A \nlarge scale upper level trough looks to build across the eastern \nCONUS, with surface low pressure centered in the Ohio Valley. As the \ninitial low moves eastward, a secondary coastal low is expected to \ndevelop and strengthen, which will bring our chances for widespread \nprecipitation. Although there is still some uncertainty regarding \nthe exact timing and track of the system, it looks to be fairly \nprogressive and consensus continues to grow amongst guidance as we \nget closer. Some of the biggest uncertainty has been and continues \nto be on expected precipitation type with this event. Higher \nelevations can expected to receive some wet snow accumulations with \nthis event, but valley rain is expected for the most part, \nespecially with the lack of cold air initially. Some snow may mix in \nat the lower elevations, particularly Thursday night into Friday \nmorning, but accumulations are not expected. Another area of \nuncertainty is how quickly cold air advects into the region which \ncould allow for some additional snowfall on the backside of the \nsystem, although there is still some uncertainty on amounts. As of \nnow, snowfall totals look to be between 1-4 inches across the High \nPeaks of the Adirondacks and the spine of the Green Mountains \nThursday and Friday. As previously mentioned, there is still some \nuncertainty, so while confidence has increased there are still some \ndetails that change over the next day or so. Temperatures are expect \nto trend cooler towards the end of the week, with highs generally in \nthe 30s to low 40s by Friday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: Upper level troughing will prevail for much of the \nweekend, leading to a mostly cloudy and cool stretch. Friday night, \nsurface low pressure will be tracking northeastward across the \nAtlantic, resulting in northwesterly flow for northern New York and \nVermont and potential for upslope showers, 30-65% chance \nprecipitation Friday night and Saturday. As temperatures fall from \nthe 30s at the start of Friday to the seasonably cold upper \nteens/20s by early Saturday morning, any potential precipitation \nwould start as snow at higher elevations, while wider valleys would \nsee rain become rain/snow mix or even snow as temperatures fall \ntheir lowest. Showers may linger into Sunday at higher elevations, \nlikely drying out by Monday. Snow levels rise again each day as \nhighs reach a seasonably cool mid 30s and 40s, but are expected to \nfall each night to around 1000 feet or even lower as temperatures \ndip into the upper teens to lower 30s. Several inches will be \npossible in the mountains, but anything in the valleys would likely \nbe lighter and have a hard time accumulating. Heading into next \nweek, a large surface high pressure looks to build into the region, \nbringing increasing sunshine and cold overnight lows.",
